# ssh allowed_signers — the humans who may grant HG1 and HG2.
#
# SPEC.md §7.3 defines exactly two human gates in the "SEC Regulation
# Crowdfunding: go" pipeline:
#
#   HG1  after P5, before P6's tests are treated as specifications
#        — domain-expert review of the inert-style L4
#   HG2  anything outward-facing — creating the corpus repo, publishing the
#        report, ANY lexipedia contact (including after R2's read-only probe)
#
# etc/go/gate-verify.sh is the only reader of this file. It runs:
#
#   ssh-keygen -Y verify -f <this file> -I <identity> \
#              -n l4-go-gate|l4-go-gate-hg2 -s <sig> < <payload>
#
# THIS FILE SHIPS EMPTY OF KEYS, DELIBERATELY. No signer is enrolled, so every
# gated stage refuses with exit 3 and says why. The orchestrator does not
# invent an approver, and it does not carry a key it was not given.
#
# To enrol, append one line in ssh allowed_signers format:
#
#   mengwong@gmail.com ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA… comment
#
# and grant a gate by signing the requested payload out of band, with a private
# key that never enters this worktree:
#
#   etc/go/gate-request.sh HG1 --run <rundir>
#   ssh-keygen -Y sign -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 -n l4-go-gate <rundir>/HG1.payload.txt
#
# Agents can verify and cannot sign. Until a key is enrolled here, the only way
# past HG1 is an explicit, recorded waiver:
#
#   etc/go/go.sh run … --waive HG1="reason this run may proceed unsigned"
#
# which lands on the journal as a gate record with state=waived and prints in
# the report's Gates section. A waiver that is not in the report is impossible,
# and a waiver is bound to the sha256 of the corpus files it was granted over:
# edit one and HG1 closes again, for that run and every resume of it.
#
# THERE IS NO WAIVER ROUTE PAST HG2. Its subject is anything outward-facing,
# which is Meng's decision and not an agent's, so `go.sh run --waive HG2=…`
# exits 2. HG2 opens on a signature or not at all.
